Original problem:

Power cycle caused the following error message to appear:

Error (ena0), Network station address ROM has a bad checksum.

Resolution:

At the firmware prompt, show config lists a device in isa slot 1
named DE200-LE.  This device is not present in the machine.
(I'm not certain if this is a firmware issue or a hardware issue.)

To remove the offending device, re-initialize the ISA with:

>>>isacfg -init

To then save the changes, re-initialize the firmware

>>>init

The problem I was experiencing occured only at power cycle, though.
The device kept re-appearing.  The firmware configurations were
not being saved.  Time to replace the ROM chip. 

Turns out this machine has other problems too....

